  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Some stronger showers and storms possible this afternoon and\n   again Sunday afternoon.\n   \n\n-  Significant rain event for much of the Northern Rockies Sunday\n   through Tuesday morning.\n\n-  Gradual drying trend develops mid-week alongside morning \n   valley fog.\n\nA closed low pressure circulation is visible this afternoon on\nsatellite imagery along the Washington/Oregon border. This low\nwill continue to send waves of energy into western Montana and\nnorth central Idaho, increasing shower and thunderstorm activity \nthrough the afternoon. A few isolated strong storms are possible,\nmainly across southwest Montana.\n\nA defined area of showers with embedded thunder is anticipated to\ndevelop overnight into early Sunday morning as the low pressure \nsystem slowly wobbles eastwards. Persistent rain is anticipated\ngenerally along the Highway 93 corridor.  \n\nAs the upper low begins to track into the Northern Rockies Sunday,\nupper level forcing in the atmosphere will combine with\nsignificant moisture advecting into the region to bring another\nround of showers and a few strong to severe storms. These storms\nwill be capable of very heavy rainfall rates (0.75 inches per hour\nor greater) along with the potential for hail and wind gusts up to\n50 mph. The heavy rain in particular could cause localized\nflooding issues, particularly if a flood prone area or burn scar\ntakes a direct hit. Storms will be possible across the entire\nregion, but the stronger storms are more likely across west-\ncentral and southwest Montana.  \n\nBy late Sunday night into Monday, widespread moderate to heavy\nrainfall will develop across western Montana and north central\nIdaho. The probability for half an inch of rain by Tuesday \nmorning is generally 80 to 100 percent for most locations, other\nthan the southern end of Lemhi County. The most significant\nrainfall is anticipated generally from Missoula county north and\neast, including Lake and Flathead counties. Recent model trends\nshow widespread amounts of 1 to 3 inches of rainfall in these\nareas, with up to 3 inches along the Continental Divide, \nincluding over Glacier National Park. There is even a low\nprobability of 4 inches or more along the higher terrain near the\nDivide, including the Mission, Swan and Flathead Ranges. For the\nmost part, this is expected to be a beneficial rainfall event.\nHowever, locations that receive the higher end totals may see\nrapid rises on small streams and creek, rock and debris slides\nonto roads, and minor flooding in poor drainage areas. \n\nSome model trends indicate that the low may be slower to exit the\nregion, possibly lingering rainfall into Tuesday. It does look\nlike a general drying trend will follow later Tuesday into mid\nweek though a generally northwest flow aloft may allow for\ncontinued periods of afternoon showers. Warming will also occur, \nbut it will likely only be to near normal for this time of year. \nLingering boundary-layer moisture will continue to support \novernight and early morning valley fog and low stratus.",
